{"name":"Tendril","hex":"#869c65","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tendril","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thin, spirally coiling stem that attaches a plant to its support.","A hair-like tentacle."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the shape or properties of a tendril; thin and coiling; entwining."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tendril"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tendril","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a tendril is a specialized stem, leaf or petiole with a thread-like shape used by climbing plants for support and attachment, as well as cellular invasion by parasitic plants such as Cuscuta. There are many plants that have tendrils; including sweet peas, passionflower, grapes and the Chilean glory-flower. Tendrils respond to touch and to chemical factors by curling, twining, or adhering to suitable structures or hosts. Tendrils vary greatly in size from a few centimeters up to 27 inches for Nepenthes harryana. The chestnut vine can have tendrils up to 20.5 inches in length. Normally there is only one simple or branched tendril at each node, but the aardvark cucumber can have as many as eight."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Vine.jpg/330px-Vine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tendril"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Tendril_of_climbing_plant.jpg/500px-Tendril_of_climbing_plant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Tendril_of_climbing_plant.jpg/1280px-Tendril_of_climbing_plant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Tendril_of_climbing_plant.jpg/500px-Tendril_of_climbing_plant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail